# Task 04 — Independent Scroll Sidebar

**Category:** Layout & Structure
**Difficulty:** advanced
**Style school:** Minimalism

## The problem

A fixed sidebar and a main content area that scroll independently of each
other — the sidebar stays visible while the main content scrolls on its own.

## Constraints

- Only `sticky` and `overflow` may be used to achieve this — no
  `position: fixed`.

## Key implementation decisions

- The sidebar uses `sticky top-0 h-screen overflow-y-auto`, but only from
  `md:` up. `sticky` keeps it pinned during scroll without removing it from
  the document flow (unlike `fixed`), `h-screen` gives it a fixed viewport
  height, and `overflow-y-auto` lets the sidebar itself scroll independently
  if its own content grows taller than the screen.
- On mobile, none of that applies (`grid-cols-1` stacks the sidebar above the
  main content), so `sticky`/`h-screen` are scoped behind `md:` — applying
  them unconditionally would make the sidebar awkwardly pin itself across
  the full mobile viewport instead of behaving like normal stacked content.
- `fillerItems` is a simple generated array used only to produce enough
  content blocks to make the page tall enough to actually test scrolling.

